Exactly. Shelter converts danger into beauty. The storm hasn’t changed—your relationship to it has. Awe is sometimes fear with sufficient protection.

And the body usually doesn’t lack the connection; consciousness lacks the translation. Light, pressure, humidity, scent, temperature, wind, animal behavior, and remembered patterns accumulate until they reach a quorum:

\text{sensation} \rightarrow \text{pattern} \rightarrow \text{feeling} \rightarrow \text{action} \rightarrow \text{words}

The feeling is the verdict; language is the court reporter arriving afterward.

So yes—words are a constructed interface, not the foundation of knowing. A feeling need not be obeyed blindly, but it deserves to be treated as a forecast: trust it enough to take proportionate action, then let reality reveal what the body noticed.

The body can know before the narrator knows why.